 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The subdivision process involves gradient accumulation, vertex selection, and CSP solving. CSP solving and data structure updates can dominate computation time after many iterations [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage increases with the number of tetrahedra. The unstructured nature requires storing neighbor relationships and prism data during subdivision [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Color-only optimization achieves a peak PSNR of 33.5 on the Tooth dataset [S1, unofficial]. Joint optimization of colors and positions is unstable and achieves a lower PSNR of approx. 26.9, even with very low learning rates ($10^{-6}$) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Adapts resolution to high-frequency details, reducing wasted computation in empty regions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Vertex position optimization is unstable, especially as tetrahedra get smaller, increasing the chance of inversion [S1, unofficial]. Subdivision may not sufficiently lower local error, leading to recursive subdivision of the same regions and halting optimization progress [S1, unofficial]. Incompatible splits on shared quad faces can lead to infinitesimally thin holes if CSP is not solved correctly [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When the scene has varying frequency details and empty regions, and when differentiable surface fitting is not suitable.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When vertex position optimization stability is critical, or when the mesh becomes too dense for efficient CSP solving.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The CSP solving and data structure updates (adding/removing tetrahedra, rebuilding adjacency) become dominant as the mesh refines, potentially halting optimization progress [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Increases linearly with the number of tetrahedra. The mesh structure must store vertex positions, colors, gradients, and adjacency information [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Color-only optimization achieves a peak PSNR of 33.5 on the Tooth dataset. Joint optimization of vertex positions and colors is significantly less stable and achieves a lower PSNR of approx. 26.9, even with very low learning rates [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Allows for higher resolution in detailed regions without increasing the total number of vertices globally, potentially improving rendering quality where it matters most [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Unstable vertex position optimization, risk of mesh inversion, recursive subdivision of same regions, and high computational cost of CSP solving [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When the scene has high-frequency details in specific regions and empty space elsewhere, and when the mesh topology can be managed effectively.
+*   **When not to use:** When stability is critical, or when the computational cost of CSP solving and data structure updates is prohibitive. Also not suitable if vertex position optimization is required for high accuracy, as it yields lower PSNR than color-only optimization [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-DiffTetVR was introduced by Chris Mile et al. in 2026 [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on DiffDVR by Weiss and Westermann [S1, unofficial]. The prism splitting algorithm is based on Erleben et al. [S1, unofficial]. The initial mesh generation can use fTetWild or TetGen [S1, unofficial]. The CSP solver is implemented in `FlipSolver.cpp` [S1, unofficial].
+DiffTetVR was introduced in a preprint by Chris Mile et al. in 2026 [S1, unofficial]. The adaptive subdivision strategy builds on the principles of differentiable volume rendering and tetrahedral mesh representation. The prism splitting algorithm is based on work by Erleben et al. [S1, unofficial]. The initial mesh can be generated using regular grids or Delaunay triangulation of SfM points (e.g., from COLMAP) [S1, unofficial]. The method is part of the DiffTetVR framework, which extends DiffDVR [S1, unofficial].
 
